WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:19.610
This golden algae is swimming around in water
and white light, illuminated from below using

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:21.840
a brightfield microscope.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:28.880
And here we have that mallomonas sample, filmed
a day later using a different technique thanks

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.970
to a brand new, freshly installed microscope.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:39.829
When James, our master of microscopes, saw
them for the first time, he got a little emotional

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:48.750
because, to quote him directly, “the thing
I know so well, was looking so so much different

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.089
than usual.”

00:00:53.089 --> 00:00:58.820
Those differences in how the mallomonas look
are due to a type of microscopy called

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.940
“Differential Interference Contrast.”

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.940
Or…DIC.

00:01:04.449 --> 00:01:10.300
Earlier this year, James started a crowdfunding
campaign to purchase a new microscope, one

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:18.360
that would allow him to use differential interference
contrast to produce these images you are seeing now.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:25.000
Now technically, “differential interference
contrast” is not a type of microscope.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.610
Rather, it’s a method that enhances contrast.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:37.110
And as you can see, the final product is an
image that seems almost 3-dimensional.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:42.000
Just watch this stentor coeruleus as it swims
across the slide.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:49.980
Its cilia are so prominent that it almost
feels like you can touch their vibrating fuzziness.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:56.070
And the striations down its body are so sharp
that when the stentor contracts, it’s like

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:00.460
you can feel it pushing inward and then back
out again.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:15.940
Differential interference contrast microscopy
was invented by Georges Nomarski in 1952,

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:21.890
building off the principles underlying phase
contrast microscopy, which is a different

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:24.240
though similar thing.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:30.280
But both of these methods work to translate
invisible shifts in light phase that can happen

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:36.410
when studying certain samples into visible
changes in light amplitude, which we see as

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:38.730
changes in light intensity.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:45.390
And as a result, both techniques enhance contrast
and let us see parts of the microcosmos that

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:49.290
might be invisible with regular brightfield
microscopy.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:56.250
But phase contrast microscopy and DIC shift
and work with light in different ways to accomplish

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.110
this goal.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.150
How do they do it?

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:05.160
Well, if you’ve ever taken an optics course,
you will remember how terrible that was.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:13.650
But, very basically, phase contrast and DIC
are two ways to take advantage of a strange reality.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:20.030
Light actually travels further through some
materials than others.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.060
Kinda.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:27.860
The optical path length is a function of both
the distance between two points and the refractive

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.390
index of the material the light is travelling
through.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:38.310
Basically, denser samples have longer optical
path lengths.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:43.280
Phase contrast microscopy takes advantage
of that, making areas with longer optical

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.740
path lengths look darker.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:52.690
DIC on the other hand, doesn’t make areas
that are denser darker, it uses some very

00:03:52.690 --> 00:04:01.730
cool optics to create sharp contrast in areas
of rapid change in the optical path length.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:10.650
So, the faster the gradient from more to less dense or vice versa, the more contrast you see.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:16.070
But it’s also important to note that while
these images are the result of light traveling

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:22.570
through a sample, they are not an actual topographical
map of an organism.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:29.699
DIC microscopy is a technique, but it does
require certain physical additions to a microscope.

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:32.819
For one thing, we need to split light apart.

00:04:32.819 --> 00:04:39.970
So, first we have a prism that splits polarized
light into two orthogonal rays of light that

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:45.020
will then pass through the sample and interact
with it in different ways.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:50.520
And then there’s a second prism that recombines
those two rays after they’ve traveled through

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:56.710
the sample, forming an image based on the
differences those two beams of light experienced

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:00.509
for our eyes and cameras to see.

00:05:00.509 --> 00:05:06.099
In addition to these prisms, this technique
relies on having higher quality objectives

00:05:06.099 --> 00:05:08.099
than what we’ve been using in the past.

00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:13.189
The objectives are the little lenses at the
bottom of the microscope that actually do

00:05:13.189 --> 00:05:16.430
most of the magnification.

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:21.590
Not many people would realize this, but the
objectives we use for Journey to the Microcosmos

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:29.289
are some of the cheapest and most common types
of objectives, they’re known as achromatic objectives.

00:05:29.289 --> 00:05:36.009
But while these objectives produce so many
of our favorite images, James needed a microscope

00:05:36.009 --> 00:05:40.490
update to be able to do differential interference
contrast.

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:48.110
So with help, perhaps including some from
you, James bought a new Zeiss microscope.

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:52.960
It took eight weeks for it to arrive, which
is a long time to wait.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:06:02.189
But luckily the manufacturers sent him a 150
page manual to occupy his time until it arrived.

00:06:02.189 --> 00:06:08.270
When the microscope finally showed up, James
still had to wait for an engineer to install

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.220
the nearly 50 pound machine.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:13.110
But he didn’t waste any time.

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:20.039
James went out to gather his precious Mallomonas
and prepared a slide that night, keeping it

00:06:20.039 --> 00:06:26.349
stored in a humidity chamber so that he could
look at it as soon as the microscope was ready.

00:06:26.349 --> 00:06:30.220
The next day, the Zeiss engineer took 3.5
hours

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:33.570
to put all the pieces together and set everything up.

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:40.320
But from there, well, it was all on James
to observe what he could and experiment with

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.680
his new microscope.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:03.800
Some of his experimenting has been geared
towards making sure that he can get the best

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:04.970
image possible.

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:12.099
These objectives have a much smaller working
distance compared to our previous ones, and

00:07:12.099 --> 00:07:16.389
so you need to prepare the slides a lot thinner
to get a sharper image.

00:07:16.389 --> 00:07:21.819
So even for our master of microscopes, there
was somewhat of a learning curve to make sure

00:07:21.819 --> 00:07:26.870
he was getting videos that were as sharp and
vivid as he wanted.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:32.529
But some of the experimenting is based out
of that most exciting thing of all: curiosity.

00:07:32.529 --> 00:07:38.120
He’s removed some of the prisms to see how
it affects the footage, and added a magnifying

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:42.100
glass over the light source to scatter the
light in different ways.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.080
That’s the joy of the microcosmos.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:51.349
It is so infinite.

00:07:51.349 --> 00:07:57.160
There’s it’s own objective existence,
full of so many more organisms than we’ll

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:59.379
ever be able to identify.

00:07:59.379 --> 00:08:05.860
But there’s also the infinite nature of
how we experience it, of how our own view

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:12.949
of this world is shaped by the many different
tools we use to observe it.

00:08:12.949 --> 00:08:18.569
And maybe that’s the case all the time...that
what we see is as much about how we view the

00:08:18.569 --> 00:08:21.839
world as it is about the world itself.

00:08:21.839 --> 00:08:27.710
The same thing with a different lens, a different
technique, a different base of knowledge,

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:30.159
can look completely different.

00:08:30.159 --> 00:08:35.630
As we start our third season of Journey to
the Microcosmos, we hope you will continue

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:47.660
to join us through both our new and old lenses,
as we uncover more of this hidden, unending world.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:53.970
Thanks for coming with me on this journey
as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:56.040
And guess what, we have new merchandise!

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:58.700
We have algae socks

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:04.190
We also have a new coloring book so you can
spend a little time with your favorite microbes

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:11.540
relaxing, zoning out, letting yourself just
be for a little while.

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:14.590
Thank you of course to all of these people
on the screen right now.

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:17.290
They are our patrons on Patreon.

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:20.580
These folks are what make this channel possible.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:28.260
If you’d like to join them in supporting
us, check us out at patreon.com/journeytomicro.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:32.500
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes James Weiss, you can check out

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:38.470
Jam &amp; Germs on Instagram where you can see
